# Gold-set data: review exports, candidate batches, and ingested gold sets all
# contain LiveATC-derived transcripts — LOCAL ONLY, same licensing rule as audio
# (python-legacy/dataset/README.md). The tooling (gold_builder.py, scoreboard.py)
# is tracked; the data's canonical home is the local training-data store
# (atc_training_data/). Export/candidate patterns are root-anchored (browser
# exports land at the repo root; tests legitimately use these names in tmp dirs).
# gold_testset*.jsonl stays unanchored: ingest is run from python-legacy/, so its
# output can land anywhere in the tree and must never be committed.
/corrections_v*.json
/candidates.json
gold_testset*.jsonl

lz/out/*.verified.json

# ⚠️ A STRAY .lzpack MUST NEVER REACH GIT AGAIN. `lz/.gitignore` already covers `lz/out/`, which is
# where packs are BUILT — but four of them were COPIED elsewhere and committed: the dev
# install-into-the-simulator recipe was run with no booted simulator, `xcrun simctl
# get_app_container` printed its error to stdout, and `cp` into "$(…)/Library/Application Support/lz/"
# created `ios/An error was encountered processing the command (…)/` and filled it. 344 MB on `main`
# — the single largest contributor to this repo passing GitHub's storage warning, and invisible
# because nobody reads a path that long. A directory-scoped ignore cannot catch a copy; this pattern
# is unanchored so it catches one ANYWHERE in the tree.
#
# Packs' real home is Hugging Face (`SingularityUS/commsight-lz`, classic Git-LFS) — lz/publish.py.
*.lzpack
# The one deliberate exception: a 16 KB 4-tile fixture, because a test that needs a 90 MB download
# is not a test.
!ios/ATCTranscribeTests/lz_fixture.lzpack

# ⚠️ NASR SUBSCRIPTION CACHE — 239 MB PER CYCLE, AND IT ALMOST REACHED main.
# `build_apt.py --date` caches the FAA 28-day subscription zip so a re-run after a parse failure
# does not re-download it. A `git add -A` immediately afterwards swept it into a commit; GitHub's
# 100 MB file limit was the only thing that stopped it, and that is luck, not a safeguard. This is
# the same shape as the four stray .lzpack files that DID reach main under a shell error message.
# Unanchored, so it is caught wherever --cache-dir is pointed.
.nasr-cache/
28DaySubscription_Effective_*.zip
# …and the partial. build_apt.py streams to "<dest>.part" and only renames on completion, so an
# interrupted fetch leaves a ~200 MB file the pattern above does NOT match. That is the same
# near-miss again: the only thing that stopped the completed zip reaching main was GitHub's 100 MB
# limit, which is luck rather than a guard.
*.zip.part
.part

# Generated by nav/publish_nav.py on EVERY run, including --dry-run, so a dry run left the tree
# dirty and invited an unrelated `git add -A` to commit a manifest nobody meant to publish. The
# authoritative copy is the one on HuggingFace; this is scratch.
nav/index.json
